venv/

# Runtime state — never committed.
/var/
*.jsonl
!tests/**/*.jsonl
!src/orbit/**/*.jsonl

# Model containers, adapters, routing profiles, KV snapshots.
#
# Every one of these is anchored with a leading slash so it matches only the
# working-directory artefact, not a source package that happens to share the
# name. Unanchored, `adapters/` also matched `src/orbit/adapters/` and silently
# excluded the entire adapter pipeline from the first commit — the package was
# on disk, imported fine locally, and simply was not in the repository.
/models/
/adapters/
/profiles/
/kvcache/
/corpus/

# The technical specification (v1) itself — the thing `sec 8.2` and friends point
# at. It is not ours to publish and this repository is public, so /specs/ is the
# place to drop a local copy and it never leaves the machine. It also holds the
# running state of the work: the tracker, the ordered plan, the session diaries and
# the full model write-ups, none of which a stranger reading the code needs.
#
# The rule that keeps this safe is one-directional. A file in /specs/ may cite
# anything; **a committed file may never cite /specs/**, because a citation that
# does not survive a clone is not a citation. That has been got wrong three times
# — `specs/NEXT_STEPS.md`, then a design cited from a committed doc, then three
# benchmark scripts named as the way to reproduce a published measurement — and it
# is trap 11 in `docs/architecture.md` §5. Anything a committed file needs is in
# `docs/`, written so that publishing it is fine.
/specs/
*.safetensors
*.gguf
